Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  08 MAY 98 ACCESSIONED ON  08 MAY 98 at 08 18 CLINICAL DATA  Carcinoma of the right breast                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY  RESIDUAL INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  TWO MICROSCOPIC FOCI  PRESENT ADJACENT TO THE BIOPSY CAVITY  SEE NOTE   HEALING BIOPSY CAVITY  NOTE  Examined inked resection margins are free of tumor  Definite blood vessel or lymphatic invasion is not seen  The largest focus of invasive carcinoma spans 0 6cm  LYMPH NODES  RIGHT AXILLA   THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY IN 15 OF 15 LYMPH NODES  Diagnosis by  Joseph Misdraji  MD Signed On  12 MAY 98 at 16 15                                                                                 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number  and  right breast and axillary nodes   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 0cm right breast  with an attached REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm axillary tissue with an overlying REMOVED_DATE x 10 0cm skin ellipse containing a grossly unremarkable nipple  with a 2 5cm healing scar  2 5cm superior from the nipple  The breast is inked and serially sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm biopsy cavity filled with red brown grumous material  1 8cm inferior to the overlying skin  and 0 8cm from the closest inked deep margin  The breast parenchyma surrounding the biopsy cavity is composed of more firm  fibrous tissue with areas of fat necrosis  which extend to the overlying skin  More ill defined  firm  gritty areas  are present 3 0cm inferior and 2 0cm lateral from the biopsy cavity  These more discrete  firm areas come to within 0 3cm of the closest inked deep margin  The remainder of the breast parenchyma is composed of fibrofatty tissue  Within the axillary tissue  multiple lymph nodes  ranging from REMOVED_DATE to 1 0cm in greatest dimension are identified  SECTION CODE  A D  Biopsy cavity  E   Representative sections of areas of fat necrosis with overlying skin  F K  More firm  fibrous tissue with focal gritty areas  surrounding and away from the biopsy cavity  L   Representative section of breast parenchyma away from the biopsy cavity  M Q  Representative axillary lymph nodes  Resident  SAHA SADEGHI  MD                                                                                 SOURCE CARE UNIT  MGH INPATIENT  FLR UNKNOWN  REPORTS TO  Edwin Lee Carter  M D  PART   BREAST  RIGHT   MODIFIED RADICAL MASTECTOMY , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  FinalType  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  15 REMOVED_MONTH 93 ACCESSIONED ON  15 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 13 12 CLINICAL DATA  Left breast mass                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  1  INFILTRATING DUCTAL CARCINOMA  85   GRADE REMOVED_DATE  MODERATELY DIFFERENTIATED  MULTIFOCALLY EXTENDING TO THE MARGIN OF THE SPECIMEN  INVADING LYMPHATICS AND BLOOD VESSELS  AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  15    SEE NOTE   NOTE  The largest tumor diameter is 3 7cm  Diagnosis by  Volker Nickeleit  M D  Signed On  17 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 16 28                                                                                     S PHASE ANALYSIS Addendum     BREAST   LEFT  EXCISION OR BIOPSY   BRDU UPTAKE  PERCENTAGE OF CELLS IN S PHASE   UNINTERPRETABLE SEE NOTE  NOTE  The breast tumor tissue  after being incubated in medium containing a thymidine analog  bromodeoxyuridine  was processed and stained by immunohistochemical methods to detect those cells which were replicating DNA  Positive cells  cells in S phase  were counted by microscopic visualization of the breast tissue  Results from 372 MGH cases of invasive ductal carcinoma have shown cells in S phase ranging from 0  to 40   with a mean 6 3  and median of 5  Benign lesions  and invasive lobular carcinomas have consistently shown very low counts  less than 3    The assay was repeated 3 times  background was consistently high suggesting that there was a delay in tissue transport  that air drying occured  or that the tissue was not sent on wet ice  If S phase information is desired  flow cytometry should be attempted  Added by  Ann D  Thor  M D  Signed On  10 MAY 93 at 11 09     HORMONE BINDING ASSAY Addendum     RESULTS OF HORMONE BINDING ASSAYS AS REPORTED BY DAVID T  MACLAUGHLIN  PhD   726  REMOVED_CASE_ID   estrogen receptor  Positive 46 fm mg cytosolic protein progesterone receptor  Positive 117 fm mg cytosolic protein Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  13 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 10 36     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FROZEN TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS BREAST  LEFT   The tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  the results of prospective staining for estrogen receptor protein of 100 unfixed breast tumors were compared with determinations of estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  93  of tumors that reacted with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  100  of tumors that failed to stain were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining for progesterone receptor protein of 50 breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Seventy three percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 91  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  THE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR PROGESTERONE RECEPTOR PROTEIN ARE FOR INVESTIGATIONAL USE ONLY  THEY MUST NOT BE USED FOR CLINICAL PURPOSES  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  09 REMOVED_MONTH 93 at 17 17 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name  unit number and  Left breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 9cm irregularly shaped piece of breast tissue  The specimen is inked and sectioned  revealing a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 2 0cm hard  firm  yellow tan mass that extends to the margin  A touch prep is made  Tissue is taken for BRDU  estrogen receptors  A block is saved frozen  Representative sections of the mass are submitted in A through G  Additional sections of breast tissue are submitted in H through J  Resident  Cynthia E  Flynn  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FROZEN SECTION DIAGNOSIS  1  BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY  CARCINOMA O R  Resident  C  Diana Jordan  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SURGICAL DAY CARE   ACC 3 REPORTS TO  Barbara Smith  M D  PART   BREAST  LEFT   BIOPSY , Unchecked.
Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Final Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  OPERATION DATE  31 REMOVED_MONTH 97 ACCESSIONED ON  31 REMOVED_MONTH 97 at 15 54 CLINICAL DATA   None Provided                                                                                      FINAL DIAGNOSIS     BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  AT LEAST REMOVED_DATE CM  POORLY DIFFERENTI  ATED  EXTENDING TO SEVERAL INKED SURFACES  AND WITH EXTENSIVE LYMPHATIC INVASION  SEE NOTE   NOTE  Definite blood vascular invasion is not seen  Invasive carcinoma is present throughout the entire specimen  the tumor is at least 1 5cm since the smallest specimen dimension is 1 5cm  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  HIGH GRADE  EXTENSIVE  PRESENT ASSOCIATED WITH THE INVASION COMPONENT AND LESS THAN 1 HPF FROM SEVERAL INKED SURFACES  SKIN  RIGHT BREAST   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY  NO DIAGNOSTIC ABNORMALITY RECOGNIZED  DERMAL LYMPHATIC INVASION IS NOT SEEN  Diagnosis by  Dennis Sgroi  M D  Signed On  05 AUG 97 at 16 15                                                                                     RECEPTOR IMMUNOHISTOCHEMISTRY Addendum     RESULTS OF STAINING OF FIXED TISSUE FOR HORMONE RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Right   The tumor cells do not stain for estrogen receptor protein  The tumor cells do not stain for progesterone receptor protein  See note  Note  In this laboratory  we used this antibody to stain 100 breast carcinomas prepared in the usual way  paraffin sections  and we compared the results with determinations of estrogen receptor by biochemical assay  91  of tumors that stained with this antibody were positive for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  96  of tumors that failed to stain with this antibody were negative for estrogen receptors by biochemical assay  When we compared the results of staining of paraffin sections with the results of staining of frozen sections  the predictive value of a positive result was 97  and the predictive value of a negative result was 95   In this laboratory  we also compared the results of staining of 100 routinely processed breast carcinomas with determinations of progesterone receptors by a hormone binding assay  Sixty five percent of tumors that stained with this antibody contained progesterone receptors by the biochemical assay  and 98  of tumors that failed to stain lacked the receptors  Added by  Frederick Carl Koerner  M D  Signed On  08 AUG 97 at 14 24 GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in the Frozen Section Lab   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME  patient s name and unit number  in two parts  Labeled  Skin  is a REMOVED_DATE x 0 5cm longitudinal portion of skin excised to a depth of 0 3cm  No discrete lesions are noted on the surface  The specimen is trisected and submitted entirely in cassette A  Labeled  Breast mass  is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm portion of fibroadipose tissue  which is inked and sectioned to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 4cm firm white stellate nodule  A representative section of the nodule is frozen as frozen section  1 and resubmitted in cassette K  The remainder of the nodule is submitted in cassttes B   F  The remainder of the specimen is submitted in cassettes H   J  Gross Description by  JANINE L  DUDAC Resident  RUBEN D  CARRASCO  MD     INTRA OPERATIVE DIAGNOSIS     FXDX  1  RIGHT BREAST MASS   BIOPSY  INFILTRATING AND INTRADUCTAL CARCINOMA  O R  Resident  STUART HOUSER  MD O R  Diagnosis by  Judith Ann Ferry  M D                                                                                  SOURCE CARE UNIT  SAME DAY SURGICAL UNIT    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REPORTS TO  Barbara Lynn Smith  M D  PART A  SKIN  RIGHT BREAST   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY PART B  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISIONAL BIOPSY   report_end  , Unchecked.
